"""
Examine an MCP server's metadata, tools, resources, and prompts

Speaks both eras of the Streamable HTTP transport:

  modern  2026-07-28 and later -- stateless, per-request metadata, no
          handshake. Identity and capabilities come from server/discover.
  legacy  2025-11-25 and earlier -- initialize handshake, notifications/
          initialized, Mcp-Session-Id on every subsequent request.

Default output is one section per HTTP exchange, as the exchanges happen:

  => POST tools/list                       what was asked for
  <= 200 OK  application/json              what came back
                                           (blank line, as in HTTP)
    "tools": [ ... ]                       the result, as JSON

A summary of services found closes the report. A failed probe instead
summarizes whether HTTP, JSON-RPC, or MCP gave out.

Additionally, two data streaming modes are available:

  --json       server replies as JSON, grouped under the method that asked for
               them, one entry per method: a list that arrived in pages comes
               back collated, without the spent cursor. A nextCursor that does
               survive means paging stopped early and the list is short.
               Entries keep jsonrpc and result, or error if any call failed,
               but not id, which is this tool's own counter rather than
               anything the server said. Server notifications are not replies
               and do not appear here
  --transcript every HTTP exchange as HTTP message text, the way curl -v writes
               it: "> " is what was sent, "< " is what came back, headers and
               bodies exactly as they went over the wire, nothing withheld --
               including whatever credentials the request carried

By default the probe impersonates a real client (Claude Code) so that a server
which serves auditors one set of tool descriptions and real clients another
answers as though to a real client. --identify-as-probe announces mcp-inspect
instead.

Server text can carry terminal escape sequences, so every string that reaches
the terminal is escaped first; see safe() below.
"""

